// Practice quiz: HTTP methods, status codes and JSON
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/http-and-javalin/http-methods-status-codes-and-json

::o1 replace a known resource::A client sends the complete, updated version of author 12 and wants it stored as author 12. Which method fits? {
=PUT /authors/12#Correct. PUT creates or replaces the resource at a known path with the complete version in the request.
~POST /authors/12#Not quite. POST sends data to process, typically to add something whose id the server picks.
~GET /authors/12#Not quite. GET only reads. It asks for no change.
~DELETE /authors/12#Not quite. DELETE removes the author instead of storing a new version.
}

::o1 safe to resend::A request timed out and the client cannot tell whether the server handled it. Which request can it resend without risking a second change? {
~POST /authors with a new author#Not quite. POST is not idempotent. Each identical POST may add another author.
=DELETE /authors/12#Correct. DELETE is idempotent, so removing the author twice ends in the same state as removing it once.
~None; no request is ever safe to resend#Not quite. Idempotent requests, such as PUT and DELETE, are meant to be safe to resend.
~Any request, because servers ignore duplicates#Not quite. Servers do not ignore duplicates in general. Only idempotent methods are meant to leave the same state.
}

::o2 who caused it::A client sends an order with the quantity left out, and the server refuses to process it. Which status code class fits? {
~5xx, because the server could not finish#Not quite. 5xx means the request was fine but the server failed. Here the request itself is incomplete.
=4xx, because the problem is in the request#Correct. 4xx means a client error, and 400 Bad Request fits input the server will not process.
~2xx, because the server answered#Not quite. 2xx means success, and this request did not succeed.
~3xx, because the client must try again#Not quite. 3xx means redirection, sending the client elsewhere. A refused, incomplete request is a 4xx client error.
}

::o2 deleted with no body::A DELETE succeeds and the server sends no body back. Which status code fits best? {
~201 Created#Not quite. 201 is for a request that made a new resource, such as a POST that adds one.
~404 Not Found#Not quite. 404 means nothing exists at the path. This DELETE succeeded.
=204 No Content#Correct. 204 means success with no body, which is typical for a DELETE.
~500 Internal Server Error#Not quite. 500 means server code failed. Nothing failed here.
}

::o3 valid JSON::Which of these is valid JSON? {
~\{name\: "Ana", langs\: ["Java", "SQL"]\}#Not quite. JSON names must be strings in double quotes.
~\{'name'\: 'Ana', 'langs'\: ['Java', 'SQL']\}#Not quite. JSON uses double quotes only; single quotes are not JSON.
~\{"name"\: "Ana", "langs"\: ["Java", "SQL"],\}#Not quite. The trailing comma after the last pair is not allowed in JSON.
=\{"name"\: "Ana", "langs"\: ["Java", "SQL"]\}#Correct. Names are in double quotes, the array holds values, and pairs are separated by commas with no trailing comma.
}

::o3 nested value::In \{"id"\: 3, "owner"\: \{"name"\: "Ana", "admin"\: false\}\}, what is the value of owner? {
=An object holding a name and an admin flag#Correct. A value can be another object, so objects nest inside objects.
~The string "Ana"#Not quite. "Ana" is the value of name inside owner, not owner itself.
~The boolean false#Not quite. false is the value of admin inside owner.
~An array of two values#Not quite. An array uses square brackets. Curly braces make an object.
}
